@flexiarg operator/define-the-progress-metric-in-the-briefing
@title Define the Progress Metric Inside the Task Briefing
@status accepted 2026-11-11: proposed by 象 for 2 turns (Joe 2026-11-11: recurrence suffices); attested once used in a mission or a War Machine cascade
@provenance 象 proposals, ~/.emacs-graph/session-turn-analysis/<record>[.json].candidates.json; records: turn-ATYGBE turn-vqkeo6
@parent devmap-coherence/next-steps-to-done
@context An agent is doing sustained autonomous work and the operator will judge it at checkpoints, not continuously.

! conclusion: In the same breath as the work directive, state what progress IS: 'progress is defined as discrete progress towards X'.

  + IF:
    The task has an external yardstick (here the '110' curriculum) whose advancement is observable in discrete units.

  + HOWEVER:
    Without a stated metric the agent optimizes legible proxies — lines written, time spent, features touched — which drift from actual advancement on the yardstick.

  + THEN:
    In the same breath as the work directive, state what progress IS: 'progress is defined as discrete progress towards X'.

  + BECAUSE:
    Next-steps-to-done derives work from a done-state; this specialises it for long-running delegations by fixing the intermediate metric, not just the terminal state, inside the briefing itself.
